// @check
// @error argument 1: expected f64[], got string[]
// @error argument 1: expected i32[], got string[]
// THE ONE SHAPE THAT MUST STAY REFUSED, and the reason the D16 fix is an INFERENCE and not a
// guess: an un-annotated `return []` takes its element rep from the slot the hole meets, so a
// hole that meets TWO DIFFERENT slots has no single answer.
//
// It fails at the CHECKER, before the emitter is ever asked. The producer's return type is
// ONE inferred type; the first call unifies the hole with that call's parameter, and the
// second call then fails ordinary argument assignability. That is the whole guarantee the fix
// needs at this shape — the compiler must never silently pick one of the two reps — and it is
// a property of the checker's unification, not of the two emit-side readers the fix repaired.
// Pinned here because "the hole takes its rep from the consumer" reads, wrongly, as though a
// SECOND consumer would quietly re-seed it.
//
// The diagnostic names the FIRST slot as the producer's type (`got string[]`), which is the
// honest report: by the time the second call is checked, `fq`'s return type is `string[]`.
// A user who wants both must annotate the producer, or write two.
//
// The legs:
//   L1 two DISTINCT-BACKING reps (`string[]` then `f64[]`).
//   L2 the same against the DEFAULT i32 backing (`string[]` then `i32[]`), which is the leg
//      that would break first if the fix ever fell back to "the i32 list is always safe".

function sinkS(xs: string[]) { print(xs.length) }
function sinkF(xs: f64[]) { print(xs.length) }
function sinkI(xs: i32[]) { print(xs.length) }

function fqA() { return [] }
sinkS(fqA())
sinkF(fqA())

function fqB() { return [] }
sinkS(fqB())
sinkI(fqB())
